{"chunk_id": "a38791d8ffa1", "text": "Arjuna argues that wealth is the foundation of worldly and religious life. According to this view, prosperity enables sacrifice, duty, reputation, and social bonds; poverty, by contrast, isolates and disables action. Kings therefore must seek and accumulate wealth\u2014even through conflict\u2014because royal power, sacrifice, and the welfare of subjects depend on it. Citing the conduct of gods and ancient kings, the speaker frames conquest and royal taxation as part of a cosmic and Vedic order. Yudhishthira responds by rejecting this logic. Instead of ruling through power and wealth, he declares his intention to renounce the world entirely. He describes in vivid detail a life of forest asceticism: living on fruits and roots, practicing austerities, harming no creature, and abandoning pride, desire, and social identity. The passage contrasts two ideals\u2014kingly duty rooted in wealth and renunciation rooted in spiritual detachment.\nArjuna argues wealth and royal duty justify power and sacrifice; Yudhishthira rejects it, declaring he will renounce kingship for austere forest asceticism.\nThemes: wealth and dharma, kingship vs renunciation, ascetic ideal, power and morality, inner detachment", "vector": [0.011174080893397331, -0.016930829733610153, -0.0002447865845169872, 0.0022078626789152622, -0.013303745537996292, 0.019419874995946884, -0.035086214542388916, -0.010495251044631004, -0.02398534119129181, -0.025343002751469612, 0.009377176873385906, 0.00861182902008295, 0.014148956164717674, 0.0035405666567385197, -0.011327151209115982, 0.014162266626954079, 0.05845927819609642, -0.023825617507100105, 0.008352275937795639, -0.003676998196169734, -0.02033829130232334, -0.009616764262318611, -0.016464965417981148, -0.009902938269078732, 0.012911088764667511, 0.021363193169236183, 0.010661630891263485, -0.02048470638692379, 0.01772945374250412, -0.019872428849339485, -0.008139310404658318, -0.009337246417999268, 1.1224151421629358